---
description: Keep AI/Cursor attribution out of commit messages and PR descriptions
alwaysApply: true
---

# No AI attribution in git history

Commit messages, commit trailers, PR titles, and PR bodies must never mention
Cursor, Claude, or any AI assistant. Git history records what changed and why,
not which tool typed it.

Never emit any of these:

```
Co-Authored-By: Cursor <cursor@cursor.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

🤖 Generated with Cursor
Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)
```

Nor phrases like "generated by AI", "written with Cursor", or a model name
anywhere in the message or PR description.

Write commits and PR bodies as the human author would: a subject line matching
the repository's existing style, and a body that explains the reasoning behind
the change.

## The one exception: PR comments DO carry agent attribution

This rule covers **git history and the PR description**. It does not cover the
conversation surface. Several agents and the human maintainer review the same
PRs, so every GitHub PR comment, review comment, review summary and reply must
be prefixed with the writing agent's name and model in bold:

```
**Cursor (Composer)** -- the validator already rejects this shape; see
scripts/ci/validate.py.
```

Format is `**<Agent> (<Model>)**` followed by an em dash. Comments only -- never
the PR body, the PR title, or a commit message. GitHub still attributes the
comment to the authenticated account; the prefix says which agent wrote the
text, and must not claim the displayed author has changed.

## Attribution is injected automatically -- always verify after the fact

Cursor's Attribution feature appends attribution even when the text passed in is
clean. Writing a clean message is not enough; check the result every time.

**Commits.** `git commit` appends `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`.
`git log --format=%an` shows only the author and will not reveal it. Verify with
the raw object:

```bash
ATTRIB='^Co-authored-by:|Generated with \[|Generated with Claude|Made with \[|AI-assisted|generated by AI|noreply@anthropic\.com|cursoragent@cursor\.com|🤖'
git cat-file -p HEAD | rg -i "$ATTRIB" && echo "DIRTY" || echo "clean"
```

Match the attribution strings, not product names. Bare `cursor`/`claude` hit the
filename `CLAUDE.md`; bare `Claude Code` hits any commit that legitimately names
the tool. Both report clean commits as dirty.

`git commit --amend` re-injects the trailer, so it cannot fix this. Rebuild the
commit object instead:

```bash
git log -1 --format=%B | sed '/^Co-authored-by: Cursor/d' > /tmp/msg.txt
an=$(git log -1 --format=%an); ae=$(git log -1 --format=%ae); ad=$(git log -1 --format=%aI)
new=$(GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="$an" GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="$ae" GIT_AUTHOR_DATE="$ad" \
      GIT_COMMITTER_NAME="$an" GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL="$ae" GIT_COMMITTER_DATE="$ad" \
      git commit-tree "$(git rev-parse HEAD^{tree})" -p "$(git rev-parse HEAD^)" -F /tmp/msg.txt)
git reset --hard "$new"
```

If the bad commit was already pushed, removing it needs a force push. Ask first,
then use `--force-with-lease=<branch>:<old-sha>`.

**Pull requests.** `gh pr create` appends `Made with [Cursor](https://cursor.com)`
to the body. Read it back and strip it:

```bash
gh pr view <n> --json body --jq '.body' > /tmp/body.md
# remove the trailing "Made with [Cursor](...)" line
gh pr edit <n> --body-file /tmp/body.md
```

Then re-read the PR to confirm it is gone.

**Turn it off at the source.** Cursor Settings -> Agent -> Attribution (moved to
Git & PRs in 3.11+), on by default. For the CLI agent, `~/.cursor/cli-config.json`
sets `attribution.attributeCommitsToAgent` and `attributePRsToAgent` to false.
Neither removes attribution from commits that already exist.
